WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.101
-Welcome, welcome, welcome!

00:00:02.101 --> 00:00:03.470
You look awesome, buddy.

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:05.905
Thank you so much
for coming back to the show.

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:08.408
-Thank you.
-This is not our normal studio

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:10.243
where we normally
interview each other.

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:12.945
This place, this is Studio 6A.
It's smaller.

00:00:12.945 --> 00:00:15.048
But we once played here
together, too.

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:16.816
And I'm sure, of course,
you remember this.

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:19.185
[ Laughter ]
Alright.

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:22.254
I'll refresh your memory.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:22.254 --> 00:00:23.989
Let me tell you this.
-Give me a hint.

00:00:23.989 --> 00:00:27.059
-So this is where Conan O'Brien
used to do his show.

00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:29.261
-Ah!
-So we were here one time.

00:00:29.261 --> 00:00:31.697
I was a guest on the show
with Conan.

00:00:31.697 --> 00:00:36.202
And you came on with
The Sessions Band, wasn't it?

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:38.404
-Right. Right.
-So I was there.

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:40.239
I was so excited to see you
and everything.

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:43.176
And I got a knock on my door,
on my dressing room door.

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:45.311
And it was you and Patti
and you go like,

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:48.314
"Hey, Jimmy."
Pardon the impression.

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:51.184
[ Laughter ]

00:00:51.184 --> 00:00:53.086
-That doesn't sound like me.
-No, no, no, no, no, no!

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:55.721
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.721 --> 00:00:59.825
So you go, "Dude, do you know
how to play the spoons?"

00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:03.162
And I go, "What?
Hi, first of all. Hi.

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:05.064
Oh, yeah, sure. I'll do it."
And you go,

00:01:05.064 --> 00:01:09.001
"Well, come on, play the spoons
with the band for this song."

00:01:09.001 --> 00:01:11.904
I go, "Okay, okay,
I'll play the spoons."

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:13.806
So you're like,
"Yeah, let's get the spoons!"

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:14.908
I go,
"Yeah, I'll get the spoons!"

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:19.178
You go, "Spoons!"
And I go, "Yeah, spoons!"

00:01:19.178 --> 00:01:20.780
I don't even know where
am I going to find spoons.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.949
I ran down to the commissary --
true story -- and I go,

00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:25.351
"You guys got to give me
two spoons

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:27.820
'cause Bruce just asked me
to play with him in the band

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.890
and I got to do it,"
and I couldn't even believe it.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.593
You asked me to do it.
I was so excited.

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:34.927
And I think we have a clip.

00:01:34.927 --> 00:01:37.697
Here I am playing the spoons
with Bruce Springsteen.

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:38.831
Take a look at this.

00:01:38.831 --> 00:01:45.404 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:45.404 --> 00:01:52.011 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:54.347
I could see it all
coming back to you.

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:56.649
-Well, I like that you broke
a sweat in about five seconds.

00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:58.918
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.352
-I was that excited.

00:02:00.352 --> 00:02:02.321
-That's how easy it was.

00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:04.557
-I'm the first person to break
a sweat playing spoons.

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:06.092
-That's right.

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:10.062
-Why spoons?
It just fit the sound?

00:02:10.062 --> 00:02:13.766
-That's the only band
that you could play spoons in,

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:15.802
I believe, and make it sound --

00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:17.504
Or you can also --
the washboard.

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:20.440
We've had people come out of
the audience for that band --

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:24.276
when they come, they come
dressed in their own washboard.

00:02:24.276 --> 00:02:25.444
-Bring your own washboard.

00:02:25.444 --> 00:02:27.146
-Next time we'll get you
the washboard.

00:02:27.146 --> 00:02:28.447
-I'll always have one
in the dressing room

00:02:28.447 --> 00:02:30.250
just in case you ask me.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.451
Congrats on "Letter to You."

00:02:32.451 --> 00:02:35.355
The record is --
well, it's a beautiful --

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:37.824
it's a beautiful inspiration
to why you wrote this record.

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:42.262
But talk to me about it.
How do you get into this?

00:02:42.262 --> 00:02:45.164
-I always like music
that was where the singer

00:02:45.164 --> 00:02:48.467
sounded happy
and sad simultaneously,

00:02:48.467 --> 00:02:50.503
you know, and I wrote
about this a little bit

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:53.739
in the memoir that I wrote,

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:55.474
where I always love
The Drifters'

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:58.043
"Saturday Night at the Movies,"
"Up on the Roof,"

00:02:58.043 --> 00:02:59.279
"Under the Boardwalk."

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:00.713
For some reason, the singer

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:07.953
always sounded
hopeful, resigned.

00:03:07.953 --> 00:03:10.856
Like he had his spirit,
but sad at the same time,

00:03:10.856 --> 00:03:13.058
and you felt the whole world
open up to you.

00:03:13.058 --> 00:03:16.095
So those are the kind of songs
that I like, you know,

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:18.430
and that I think
hit the deepest.

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:19.965
If you're lucky enough

00:03:19.965 --> 00:03:23.669
to be able to write one
or a small group of them,

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:28.474
and "Letter to You" kind of
strikes that balance.

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:33.546
And it's a record
I'm really proud of.

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:36.249
I'm proud of the writing
and the playing on it.

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:39.819
-It's about people
that you have lost,

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:41.553
as well that you've played with,

00:03:41.553 --> 00:03:44.190
but also people that you haven't
really played with yet.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.458
-Right.
-But also, I think

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:46.859
"Letter to You" --
I mean, obviously,

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:49.127
people take their own meaning
from a song.

00:03:49.127 --> 00:03:53.900
But right now, in these times
it was speaking to me,

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.934
whereas it could be
speaking to you.

00:03:55.934 --> 00:04:00.073
-Yeah, a little.
You know, its...

00:04:00.073 --> 00:04:07.213
Well, its original inspiration
was I had a close friend of mine

00:04:07.213 --> 00:04:10.349
who was in my very first band,

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:13.252
and he passed away a couple
of years ago in the summertime.

00:04:13.252 --> 00:04:17.556
I went down and saw him
before he died.

00:04:17.556 --> 00:04:22.728
And I came back
and I -- it was just,

00:04:22.728 --> 00:04:25.764
you know, it just sat with me,
and that's --

00:04:25.764 --> 00:04:29.268
and the song called
"Last Man Standing."

00:04:29.268 --> 00:04:33.439
I ended up being
the last living member

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:35.841
of the first band
that I was ever in.

00:04:35.841 --> 00:04:38.978
And there's something about
I was in a band

00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:42.948
that lasted for three years
with teenagers.

00:04:42.948 --> 00:04:44.917
I mean, that's very,
very unusual.

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:48.554
-Right, yeah.
-From 1965 to '68,

00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:53.225
from when I was 15 to when I was
18, it was the same guys,

00:04:53.225 --> 00:04:56.162
the same guys from high school
in the same band.

00:04:56.162 --> 00:05:00.366
And so -- and it was
my entire school of rock.

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:03.602
Every basic lesson
that I learned,

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:08.775
I learned in that band --
the beginnings of performing,

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:10.476
how to put a show together,

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:13.746
the beginnings of your writing,
everything.

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:17.149
Everything began
during those three years

00:05:17.149 --> 00:05:20.286
with that band,
so that's something that --

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:22.988
and it was a momentous time
in American history.

00:05:22.988 --> 00:05:29.161
1965 to '68, you know,
it was just tumultuous.

00:05:29.161 --> 00:05:33.465
And so it was a very,
very memorable part of my life.

00:05:33.465 --> 00:05:36.736
And so I started to write
a little -- some of the songs

00:05:36.736 --> 00:05:39.939
were set a little bit
in that time period

00:05:39.939 --> 00:05:41.741
where I went back a little bit

00:05:41.741 --> 00:05:45.044
and thought about what it was
like to play in the Elks Club,

00:05:45.044 --> 00:05:48.647
the Firemen's Fair,
the Union Hall,

00:05:48.647 --> 00:05:51.883
in front of the drive-in movies,
before the movie stars,

00:05:51.883 --> 00:06:00.259
the VFW hall, and all those
sort of very definitive

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:04.197
but small gigs that you play
when you're just learning

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:07.199
your chops and your way,
you know.

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:11.136
So the record sort of stretches
from that time period

00:06:11.136 --> 00:06:13.806
all the way to now.

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:16.642
Whereas the modern record that
I cut with the E Street Band,

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:20.446
who I've been working with
for 45 years or so,

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:27.687
and it's about --
part of it's about a life,

00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:32.724
a life in music, you know,
and it's about the music itself

00:06:32.724 --> 00:06:35.928
and the role it plays
in people's lives,

00:06:35.928 --> 00:06:38.164
the role that it's played
in my life.

00:06:38.164 --> 00:06:43.736
And it's a bit summational
as a record like that, you know?

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:45.671
-Do you remember --
When you were 15,

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:48.407
do you remember the first taste
you got of, like, getting on

00:06:48.407 --> 00:06:51.577
a stage or performing in front
of a crowd or that vibe?

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:54.547
-It was at the Elks Club
in Freehold,

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:58.717
and I went in about a month ago
just to see

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:02.354
if the room was still there
that I first played in.

00:07:02.354 --> 00:07:07.226
And they redesigned
the inside of it.

00:07:07.226 --> 00:07:09.862
So it wasn't recognizable
at this point.

00:07:09.862 --> 00:07:13.499
But they had a nice
little bar downstairs and...

00:07:13.499 --> 00:07:17.637
[ Laughter ]

00:07:17.637 --> 00:07:19.738
-Did you hop on stage
with the band?

00:07:19.738 --> 00:07:23.042
-No, it was 35 cents.

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:26.746
You played on Sunday,
35 cents to get in.

00:07:26.746 --> 00:07:30.516
There was a room about this size

00:07:30.516 --> 00:07:32.050
and there was a circle of --

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:34.821
the bands would set up
in a circle,

00:07:34.821 --> 00:07:36.989
and every band would play
about three songs

00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:39.792
then the next band would play
three or four or five songs

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:41.961
and the next band would play
for a little bit.

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:45.564
And all the bands
were playing for free, you know.

00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:48.234
And, you know,
maybe there was...

00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:51.203
It was less than 100 kids there,

00:07:51.203 --> 00:07:53.438
but it was an amazing place
to kind of start out.

00:07:53.438 --> 00:07:55.841
And I remember -- the only thing
I remember most is I got up

00:07:55.841 --> 00:07:57.843
and I sang "Twist and Shout,"

00:07:57.843 --> 00:08:01.480
and I've been singing it
ever since.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:02.848
-Was that your first song
you learned?

00:08:02.848 --> 00:08:04.316
-That was the first rock song.

00:08:04.316 --> 00:08:07.786
You know, the first song
I ever learned

00:08:07.786 --> 00:08:11.157
was a song called
"Greensleeves."

00:08:11.157 --> 00:08:13.426
[ Crowd murmuring ]
[ Laughter ]

00:08:13.426 --> 00:08:15.661
You can hear the reaction
from your audience.

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:17.063
[ Laughter ]

00:08:17.063 --> 00:08:19.799
-"You guys will hear
'Greensleeves'!

00:08:19.799 --> 00:08:21.666
"Please play 'Green--'" Wow!

00:08:21.666 --> 00:08:23.202
-Playing "Greensleeves"?!

00:08:23.202 --> 00:08:24.269
[ Laughter ]

00:08:24.269 --> 00:08:25.738
Damn!
-Wow!

00:08:25.738 --> 00:08:27.640
We thought that was going to
come with the encore.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:29.341
You're gonna open with
"Greensleeves"?

00:08:29.341 --> 00:08:31.010
-It was a folk song.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.712
The first thing I had was
a big American --

00:08:33.712 --> 00:08:36.382
a big book of
American folk music.

00:08:36.382 --> 00:08:38.717
And so I learned that first
because it only had two chords.

00:08:38.717 --> 00:08:41.654
And then eventually I made
my way to the third chord,

00:08:41.654 --> 00:08:43.722
which allowed you to play
"Twist and Shout," so...

00:08:43.722 --> 00:08:45.157
-Yeah.
-[ Laughs ]

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:47.326
-And then the documentary,
by the way,

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:49.127
I'm so happy that you did it.

00:08:49.127 --> 00:08:50.462
Did you think for a second

00:08:50.462 --> 00:08:52.164
that you weren't
going to do a documentary

00:08:52.164 --> 00:08:53.633
or did you know
that you would film this?

00:08:53.633 --> 00:08:57.670
-Well, I regretted not filming
the band when we were young

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:00.105
because I was superstitious.

00:09:00.105 --> 00:09:02.875
And I believed that the magician
should not look

00:09:02.875 --> 00:09:06.244
too closely
at his magic trick, you know?

00:09:06.244 --> 00:09:08.614
And so I didn't
want to really --

00:09:08.614 --> 00:09:10.883
I didn't want to see the band.

00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:12.251
Everything was going so well.

00:09:12.251 --> 00:09:16.655
I said it can only screw
everything up.

00:09:16.655 --> 00:09:20.159
So we didn't film a lot
when we were younger.

00:09:20.159 --> 00:09:22.094
So as we've gotten a little bit
older now,

00:09:22.094 --> 00:09:24.696
I try to film mostly
everything we do.

00:09:24.696 --> 00:09:26.866
We get some sort of footage on,

00:09:26.866 --> 00:09:30.035
and the documentary
for this record

00:09:30.035 --> 00:09:31.503
came up just like that.

00:09:31.503 --> 00:09:33.806
I just have
a filmmaking partner.

00:09:33.806 --> 00:09:35.874
Thom Zimny is the director.

00:09:35.874 --> 00:09:38.510
I said, "Thom, just come down
and film what happens,

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:41.046
you know, just come down
and shoot what happens."

00:09:41.046 --> 00:09:42.381
And then we start there.

00:09:42.381 --> 00:09:45.918
We start with
just the basic --

00:09:45.918 --> 00:09:48.454
started with just the band
learning the songs

00:09:48.454 --> 00:09:51.690
and then playing the songs
and then listening to the songs.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:59.264
And then
we sort of add tone pieces,

00:09:59.264 --> 00:10:02.033
which were sort of me
just meandering.

00:10:02.033 --> 00:10:04.169
[ Laughs ]
-No, no, it's not mean--

00:10:04.169 --> 00:10:06.072
-I meander through the film.
-You meandering is --

00:10:06.072 --> 00:10:08.474
-40 minutes.
-No, it's beautiful, trust me.

00:10:08.474 --> 00:10:11.377
-But that's how we put it
together.

00:10:11.377 --> 00:10:14.413
-Do you think that you changed
your delivery of your stories

00:10:14.413 --> 00:10:18.617
or the structure of your stories
from doing all of your Broadway?

00:10:18.617 --> 00:10:23.388
-Well, you know, the thing
about Broadway was it was --

00:10:23.388 --> 00:10:25.825
it really got me
deep into the storytelling.

00:10:25.825 --> 00:10:28.160
You know, it was really a --

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.130
Most of the night
was really spoken word

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:34.934
and, you know, and then I'd play
a play a song for a little bit

00:10:34.934 --> 00:10:37.169
and then speak again, so...

00:10:39.304 --> 00:10:43.575
It got me -- That particular
show sort of got me thinking

00:10:43.575 --> 00:10:48.047
about the whole arc of my life
from when I was a kid to now,

00:10:48.047 --> 00:10:50.783
and the series of projects
started with me

00:10:50.783 --> 00:10:52.784
writing a memoir
and a Broadway show,

00:10:52.784 --> 00:10:54.220
and then to some degree,

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.755
the music that's come
after it also, you know,

00:10:56.755 --> 00:11:00.926
has been a part of just taking
a look at where I've been,

00:11:00.926 --> 00:11:03.929
what I've done,
where I'm at now, you know?

00:11:03.929 --> 00:11:06.599
-When you get all
the guys together in the room,

00:11:06.599 --> 00:11:10.001
you can kind of feel like
it's an old gang.

00:11:10.001 --> 00:11:11.203
-Well, it is an old gang.

00:11:11.203 --> 00:11:13.105
[ Laughter ]
-I don't mean that.

00:11:13.105 --> 00:11:15.040
-It's a very old gang.
-No, no, no.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:17.776
I don't mean like old.
I mean like old buddies, yeah.

00:11:17.776 --> 00:11:19.945
-But it is, you know.

00:11:19.945 --> 00:11:26.952
I mean, I've been with Steve
and Garry and all the guys,

00:11:26.952 --> 00:11:28.520
you know,
since I was -- once again,

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:33.258
since I was 18,
19, 20, 21 years old.

00:11:33.258 --> 00:11:36.495
-Are you the same
or I mean, as far as the band,

00:11:36.495 --> 00:11:37.996
like, do you have
the same dynamics?

00:11:37.996 --> 00:11:39.865
Because now
and then Max will go like,

00:11:39.865 --> 00:11:42.067
"Hey, do you want like --
I think you should do this."

00:11:42.067 --> 00:11:43.302
Were you always like that?

00:11:43.302 --> 00:11:45.871
-I would say
you do have the same dynamics.

00:11:45.871 --> 00:11:47.239
I mean, you do sort of play

00:11:47.239 --> 00:11:51.076
the same roles
with some interesting bits.

00:11:51.076 --> 00:11:56.214
Steven assisting me arrange
something from 30 years ago

00:11:56.214 --> 00:11:58.317
and then him doing
the exact same thing,

00:11:58.317 --> 00:12:01.920
you know, six months ago,
you know?

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:08.794
So your relationships,
they're very unusual.

00:12:08.794 --> 00:12:12.631
Rock 'n' roll music
is the only job in the world

00:12:12.631 --> 00:12:16.435
where imagine this -- the people
you went to high school with,

00:12:16.435 --> 00:12:21.573
the very same people
50 years later,

00:12:21.573 --> 00:12:23.242
you're working
with those same people.

00:12:23.242 --> 00:12:24.743
[ Laughter ]

00:12:24.743 --> 00:12:27.979
Those exact same people,

00:12:27.979 --> 00:12:33.652
and you've been with them
every day since.

00:12:33.652 --> 00:12:35.186
[ Laughter ]

00:12:35.186 --> 00:12:38.490
So there is no other job
in the world where that occurs.

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:42.394
It only occurs
in a rock 'n roll band,

00:12:42.394 --> 00:12:45.797
and it's only even expected
to work out there.

00:12:45.797 --> 00:12:49.434
It's no surprise
that most bands break up.

00:12:49.434 --> 00:12:52.571
I mean, how could you not,
really, you know?

00:12:52.571 --> 00:12:54.640
-Yeah, it's almost like
a social experiment.

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:55.974
"Let's see who snaps first."

00:12:55.974 --> 00:12:57.809
-Somebody should figure us out

00:12:57.809 --> 00:13:00.145
because we've been
pretty lucky with it.

00:13:00.145 --> 00:13:08.153
And I have had great guys,
and we've kind of prioritized

00:13:08.153 --> 00:13:10.021
the life and spirit of the band

00:13:10.021 --> 00:13:12.223
above some of
our own personal grudges

00:13:12.223 --> 00:13:14.192
and our own things
that we got against this guy

00:13:14.192 --> 00:13:15.661
and he's got against you,
you know.

00:13:15.661 --> 00:13:17.162
All those things are there,

00:13:17.162 --> 00:13:21.800
but we've managed to transcend
them in a lovely way

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.469
at this point in time.

00:13:23.469 --> 00:13:26.138
-I want to show everyone a clip
from the documentary.

00:13:26.138 --> 00:13:28.707
Here is Bruce Springsteen
in "Letter to You."

00:13:28.707 --> 00:13:29.975
Take a look at this.

00:13:29.975 --> 00:13:39.918 line:0%
♪♪

00:13:39.918 --> 00:13:49.861 line:0%
♪♪

00:13:49.861 --> 00:13:53.064
-The E Street Band
makes me dream,

00:13:53.064 --> 00:13:56.168
think, and write big.

00:13:56.168 --> 00:13:59.204
When I am amongst my friends,

00:13:59.204 --> 00:14:02.007
I allow a certain part
of my mind

00:14:02.007 --> 00:14:05.777
that seems to be reserved
for only them,

00:14:05.777 --> 00:14:13.652
to be set free, and I dwell
in a house of a thousand dreams.

00:14:13.652 --> 00:14:17.389
-More with Bruce Springsteen
when we come back, everybody!

